findings.description:
`resume_item`/`_resume_locked` are `async def`, but the code calls a dozen blocking operations straight on the loop instead of through `asyncio.to_thread`:
- `_validate` → `state_manager.load` (file read), `load_pipeline` (file + YAML parse), `BatchReport.load` (file + JSON parse);
- `_git_precondition` → `read_integration_target` (a `cf` subprocess), `current_branch` / `restore_target` / `dirty_paths` / `verify_git_state` (many `git` subprocesses, each bounded at 30s but the cf call has **no** timeout at all — `ContextForgeClient._run` uses `subprocess.run` without `timeout=`);
- `_select_item` → `evaluate_each_source` (the source itself does synchronous `cf` + per-slice design/review file reads) and a `git merge-base` call;
- `_reconcile` / the dependency path → `run.report.write` (file writes).

The project Python rule is explicit: any sync code inside an await-able function must run in <1ms in the worst case, and "Reviewers MUST verify this." Every other blocking call in the pipeline is already routed through `asyncio.to_thread` (`branch.py`, `commit.py`, `dispatch.py`, `review.py`, and even the executor's report write at `executor.py:1459`), so this new module breaks both the <1ms guarantee and the established convention. The concrete consequence: a hung `cf` (unbounded) or slow `git` stalls the loop with no progress indicator and delays KeyboardInterrupt handling / the `finally: session.disconnect()` cleanup. Fix by wrapping the git/cf/file work (or the whole `_validate`/`_git_precondition`/`_select_item`/`report.write` blocks) in `await asyncio.to_thread(...)`.
